1
00:00:03,121 --> 00:00:09,144
[SPEAKER_00]: So, we'll do a Madhyamanika 15 today, in terms of our little sutta study.

2
00:00:11,326 --> 00:00:21,571
[SPEAKER_00]: And that sutta, it's called the Anumāna Sutta, or Evacuation, how it's translated.

3
00:00:23,092 --> 00:00:28,155
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, it's about when Venerable Mahamoggalana was staying near the

4
00:00:30,807 --> 00:00:33,409
[SPEAKER_00]: Near the land of buggers at Crocodile Hill in the Deer Park.

5
00:00:34,930 --> 00:00:50,280
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he was talking to the to the bhikkhus about the nature of admonishment and how some bhikkhus might be hard to admonish and having qualities that keep him that way.

6
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:57,105
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, he explained on those qualities and then obviously continued in talking of how to abandon them.

7
00:00:58,308 --> 00:01:07,154
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... so the sutta goes, suppose a bhikkhu invites other bhikkhus to admonish him, but he's hard to admonish, having qualities that make him hard to admonish.

8
00:01:07,274 --> 00:01:16,960
[SPEAKER_00]: He's not forbearing, does not take instructions respectfully, so his spiritual companions don't think it's worth advising or instructing him and they don't regard him as trustworthy.

9
00:01:21,232 --> 00:01:23,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Firstly, a bhikkhu has evil wishes.

10
00:01:23,753 --> 00:01:28,755
[SPEAKER_00]: Having fallen under the sway of evil wishes, this is the quality that makes him difficult to admonish.

11
00:01:29,255 --> 00:01:30,096
[SPEAKER_00]: So, evil wishes.

12
00:01:30,216 --> 00:01:32,937
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, bhikkhu glorifies himself, puts others down.

13
00:01:33,877 --> 00:01:36,418
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, he's prone to anger, overcome by anger.

14
00:01:36,638 --> 00:01:39,319
[SPEAKER_00]: He's prone to anger, resentful due to anger.

15
00:01:39,599 --> 00:01:44,041
[SPEAKER_00]: He's stubborn due to anger, blurts out words bordering on anger.

16
00:01:44,321 --> 00:01:46,302
[SPEAKER_00]: He objects the accuser when he's accused.

17
00:01:47,082 --> 00:01:48,903
[SPEAKER_00]: When accused, he rebukes the accuser.

18
00:01:49,043 --> 00:01:51,064
[SPEAKER_00]: When accused, he retorts to the accuser.

19
00:01:51,404 --> 00:01:56,966
[SPEAKER_00]: When accused, he dodges the issue, distracts the discussion with irrelevant points, gets flustered and displays hate and bitterness.

20
00:01:57,767 --> 00:02:00,008
[SPEAKER_00]: When accused, he's unable to account for the evidence.

21
00:02:01,393 --> 00:02:07,877
[SPEAKER_00]: He denigrates and is contentious, he's jealous and stingy, devious, deceitful, stubborn and arrogant.

22
00:02:07,897 --> 00:02:13,639
[SPEAKER_00]: That all obviously results in then that furthermore that Bhikkhu is attached to his own views, holding them tight, refusing to let go.

23
00:02:14,740 --> 00:02:17,401
[SPEAKER_00]: That too is the quality that makes him difficult to admonish.

24
00:02:18,262 --> 00:02:24,525
[SPEAKER_00]: The reason why I wanted to do this sutta is basically you could simply replace

25
00:02:28,632 --> 00:02:36,754
[SPEAKER_00]: When the Maha Moggallāna talks about admonishment, he just replaced the word admonish with disagreed with.

26
00:02:37,694 --> 00:02:46,956
[SPEAKER_00]: So, suppose a bhikkhu basically encounters or, well, if we replace admonish with disagree with, then you realize that this whole sutta can apply to anyone.

27
00:02:47,616 --> 00:02:52,217
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not just for the bhikkhus and in terms of their vinaya, admonishment and similar.

28
00:02:52,797 --> 00:02:53,357
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically

29
00:02:55,040 --> 00:03:06,523
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever somebody disagrees with you, do you have the qualities that make it hard for you to stay rational, to not lose perspective?

30
00:03:07,503 --> 00:03:12,045
[SPEAKER_00]: So, suppose a bhikkhu invites other bhikkhus to disagree with him, to find the points of disagreement.

31
00:03:12,725 --> 00:03:16,506
[SPEAKER_00]: He's hard to disagree with, having qualities that make him so.

32
00:03:18,445 --> 00:03:30,915
[SPEAKER_00]: So, from that point of view, you can then include yourself in this simply, well, in this practice, and simply start re-evaluating your own mindsets when you experience disagreement in the world.

33
00:03:31,335 --> 00:03:43,425
[SPEAKER_00]: Take yourself, for example, and just see, like, whether when you experience disagreement, when somebody disagrees with you on the level of your views, on the level of how things should be done, and similar,

34
00:03:45,483 --> 00:03:50,506
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you find that experience in and of itself uncomfortable?

35
00:03:51,566 --> 00:03:52,507
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you resisting it?

36
00:03:53,287 --> 00:03:57,870
[SPEAKER_00]: If so, you then partake in the qualities that are making you hard to admonish.

37
00:03:58,710 --> 00:04:03,833
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point here is that it's not even that you necessarily need to be in the wrong

38
00:04:06,866 --> 00:04:14,793
[SPEAKER_00]: These bhikkhus, in this instance, they might be mistakenly admonishing this bhikkhu.

39
00:04:14,853 --> 00:04:16,674
[SPEAKER_00]: They might be the ones who are factually wrong.

40
00:04:17,115 --> 00:04:19,036
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point is, it's not about that.

41
00:04:19,136 --> 00:04:32,408
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about whether you, from your individual point of view, when experiencing the experience of admonishment or disagreement, are you affected by that, regardless of whether that admonishment is right or wrong.

42
00:04:33,584 --> 00:04:36,586
[SPEAKER_00]: So, are you resisting that experience?

43
00:04:36,626 --> 00:04:37,867
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you not forbearing?

44
00:04:38,488 --> 00:04:41,730
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you not take what was said respectfully?

45
00:04:41,891 --> 00:04:46,094
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, are you losing perspective and giving in to anger?

46
00:04:46,274 --> 00:04:48,836
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what the sutta then goes on to describe.

47
00:04:49,817 --> 00:04:53,580
[SPEAKER_00]: So, see, like, what are the qualities that make you hard to disagree with?

48
00:04:55,043 --> 00:04:56,345
[SPEAKER_00]: So, evil wishes.

49
00:04:56,506 --> 00:05:01,414
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, do you have desires that are rooted in greed, aversion, distraction?

50
00:05:02,296 --> 00:05:04,159
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're still partaking in those desires,

51
00:05:05,848 --> 00:05:11,130
[SPEAKER_00]: To the extent you're partaking in them, you will be under that sway of evil wishes.

52
00:05:11,190 --> 00:05:15,651
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not like the most coarse, unwholesome, evil, horrible wishes.

53
00:05:15,691 --> 00:05:15,812
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

54
00:05:15,872 --> 00:05:19,173
[SPEAKER_00]: Anything rooted in greed, aversion, delusion, it's partaking in Māra.

55
00:05:19,213 --> 00:05:20,753
[SPEAKER_00]: It's partaking in evil, so to speak.

56
00:05:21,533 --> 00:05:27,095
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for as long as you do have such wishes, that's already one quality.

57
00:05:27,255 --> 00:05:30,536
[SPEAKER_00]: They will make you resist the experience of admonishment.

58
00:05:30,676 --> 00:05:32,497
[SPEAKER_00]: They will make you have ill will towards it.

59
00:05:34,135 --> 00:05:39,257
[SPEAKER_00]: They will make you, basically, difficult to disagree with.

60
00:05:39,377 --> 00:05:43,358
[SPEAKER_00]: The second example is a big who glorifies himself and puts others down.

61
00:05:43,479 --> 00:06:01,645
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, you could take this in this, obviously, like a very caricature manner, like some absolutely, fully, full of himself, conceited person, but the same principle remains even on the subtler levels, such as, again, do you just value your views and opinions because they're yours?

62
00:06:03,210 --> 00:06:05,291
[SPEAKER_00]: Simply for that reason, you put them first.

63
00:06:06,152 --> 00:06:14,616
[SPEAKER_00]: And because if there is a form of disagreement encountered, your first response is to put the other person down, not to hear what they have to say.

64
00:06:15,957 --> 00:06:17,138
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what leads to next.

65
00:06:17,158 --> 00:06:19,579
[SPEAKER_00]: He's prone to anger, overcome by anger.

66
00:06:19,939 --> 00:06:21,300
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you just review your mind.

67
00:06:21,540 --> 00:06:23,862
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can think this doesn't apply to me, for example.

68
00:06:23,882 --> 00:06:25,383
[SPEAKER_00]: You can say, oh, no, this doesn't apply to me.

69
00:06:26,243 --> 00:06:27,364
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't have evil wishes.

70
00:06:28,577 --> 00:06:31,198
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, okay, well, do you have any wishes rooted in sensuality?

71
00:06:31,259 --> 00:06:33,360
[SPEAKER_00]: If the answer is yes, then you do have evil wishes.

72
00:06:33,960 --> 00:06:35,401
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you can say, well, I don't get angry.

73
00:06:35,421 --> 00:06:36,241
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not prone to anger.

74
00:06:36,281 --> 00:06:36,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, sure.

75
00:06:37,182 --> 00:06:39,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Overall, you're probably not... you might not be an angry person, but...

76
00:06:41,156 --> 00:06:43,137
[SPEAKER_00]: When you experience disagreement, are you irritated?

77
00:06:43,737 --> 00:06:47,518
[SPEAKER_00]: When somebody says the displeasing words to you, whether they're right or wrong is irrelevant.

78
00:06:47,718 --> 00:06:51,800
[SPEAKER_00]: We're simply talking about being subjected to that type of experience.

79
00:06:52,540 --> 00:06:55,081
[SPEAKER_00]: And if so, then, are you prone to irritation?

80
00:06:55,661 --> 00:06:57,482
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you resentful due to anger?

81
00:06:58,222 --> 00:06:58,923
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you stubborn?

82
00:06:59,003 --> 00:07:05,405
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, do you then just double down in your points regardless of what's presented as a counter-argument and so on?

83
00:07:06,085 --> 00:07:07,246
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you blurt out words?

84
00:07:07,326 --> 00:07:10,507
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a... you can review all these things and see, has it happened?

85
00:07:11,055 --> 00:07:12,295
[SPEAKER_00]: How often has it happened?

86
00:07:12,396 --> 00:07:12,816
[SPEAKER_00]: And so on.

87
00:07:13,616 --> 00:07:16,977
[SPEAKER_00]: If so, means you still partake in that domain.

88
00:07:18,298 --> 00:07:19,998
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it goes further.

89
00:07:20,098 --> 00:07:21,899
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, when accused...

90
00:07:21,919 --> 00:07:26,221
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when disagreed with, do you object the person that disagreed with you?

91
00:07:27,501 --> 00:07:29,682
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you rebuke the person that accused you?

92
00:07:29,762 --> 00:07:31,142
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you retort to them?

93
00:07:31,683 --> 00:07:40,526
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, do you look past the fact that there is a point they're making against you and attacking them ad hominem instead?

94
00:07:41,555 --> 00:07:46,018
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when accused, he doesn't look at the accusation and present the counter-argument and so on.

95
00:07:46,339 --> 00:07:48,360
[SPEAKER_00]: No, he objects the person that accused him.

96
00:07:49,541 --> 00:07:52,443
[SPEAKER_00]: When rebuked, he... when accused, he rebukes.

97
00:07:52,483 --> 00:07:53,884
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how dare you accuse me?

98
00:07:53,945 --> 00:07:54,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Who do you think you are?

99
00:07:55,025 --> 00:07:56,767
[SPEAKER_00]: And so on.

100
00:07:57,107 --> 00:07:58,528
[SPEAKER_00]: Or he retorts to the accuser.

101
00:07:58,728 --> 00:08:00,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, he does not address the accusation.

102
00:08:01,920 --> 00:08:02,660
[SPEAKER_00]: And see, that's the point.

103
00:08:02,701 --> 00:08:11,566
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, even if the accusation is wrong, you are in wrong as well if you are looking... if you... if you don't have perspective in regard to it.

104
00:08:11,626 --> 00:08:15,128
[SPEAKER_00]: And you won't have perspective in regard to it if you're resisting the discomfort of it.

105
00:08:15,929 --> 00:08:25,995
[SPEAKER_00]: And you would be resisting the discomfort of it if you are still under the evil wishes, if you still give in to anger, if you have not purified your virtue and tamed your mind on account of it.

106
00:08:27,137 --> 00:08:47,993
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all these ad hominem attacks would be pretty much self-defense mechanisms that might not even be necessary, because you still might be in the right, like the accusation might be wrong, you might be right, but the fact is that you have a face of the accuser, as the most important, the image of the accuser is associated for you with the accusation.

107
00:08:48,574 --> 00:08:53,838
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why even your response to that is irrational.

108
00:08:54,711 --> 00:09:02,574
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not on the level of the context that matters, and it's passing the point of addressing the accusation and going after the accuser.

109
00:09:03,374 --> 00:09:04,915
[SPEAKER_00]: And that, in and of itself, makes you wrong.

110
00:09:05,775 --> 00:09:08,236
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, like, both parties can be partaking in the same.

111
00:09:08,316 --> 00:09:10,917
[SPEAKER_00]: This is... this applies to anyone, really.

112
00:09:11,237 --> 00:09:13,098
[SPEAKER_00]: The one that's accusing, or the accuser.

113
00:09:13,118 --> 00:09:15,399
[SPEAKER_00]: The one that's disagreeing with, or the disagreed with.

114
00:09:16,572 --> 00:09:25,257
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if you are honest, you need to re-evaluate your own mind and simply, as I said, not look for the instances when you were accused and how you act, just overall.

115
00:09:26,078 --> 00:09:29,260
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I have wishes of sense... of sense desires?

116
00:09:30,321 --> 00:09:32,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I act out of irritation in regard to this and that?

117
00:09:32,962 --> 00:09:33,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I get ill-will?

118
00:09:33,943 --> 00:09:35,784
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I get upset?

119
00:09:35,864 --> 00:09:36,885
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I give in to anger?

120
00:09:37,745 --> 00:09:39,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Is my behavior purified?

121
00:09:39,206 --> 00:09:40,307
[SPEAKER_00]: Are my senses tamed?

122
00:09:40,507 --> 00:09:42,588
[SPEAKER_00]: If not, you are partaking in this.

123
00:09:43,509 --> 00:09:45,270
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you would have learned the perfect

124
00:09:46,345 --> 00:09:55,072
[SPEAKER_00]: way of psychotherapeutical management of your expression and always speaking softly and everything else, you're still doing that out of your will.

125
00:09:55,412 --> 00:09:57,774
[SPEAKER_00]: You're doing that out of resent, out of stubbornness.

126
00:09:57,794 --> 00:09:59,996
[SPEAKER_00]: You're doing that aimed at the accuser.

127
00:10:00,136 --> 00:10:07,262
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you cannot have the right perspective, no matter how hard you try, because all these things are covering it up and distorting it.

128
00:10:08,249 --> 00:10:16,859
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if then that person is not... the sutta goes on, says... Again, just... you can simply look at it and it's like, okay, even if you're not engaging with the ad hominem attacks,

129
00:10:29,488 --> 00:10:37,193
[SPEAKER_00]: You ask yourself, are you, first and foremost, do you immediately go on a defensive mode when there is a disagreement presented at you?

130
00:10:38,074 --> 00:10:39,795
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already irrational.

131
00:10:40,515 --> 00:10:43,617
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you have no reason to go on a defensive.

132
00:10:43,637 --> 00:10:47,000
[SPEAKER_00]: You have no reason to to immediately feel the pressure of defense.

133
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:54,525
[SPEAKER_00]: You will feel it for as long as you're not free from desires, for as long as you're not free from from from ill will, to a greater degree.

134
00:10:55,580 --> 00:11:14,853
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's the discomfort of that experience that you resist, which then projected either on the accuser, or avoiding the issue, or either way, you're going to be wiggling left and right, basically, trying to avoid, to address it, because that would kind of make it basically... well, make you feel the full weight of it.

135
00:11:15,774 --> 00:11:20,497
[SPEAKER_00]: But the catch there is that the more you resist it, the more unpleasant it is.

136
00:11:21,297 --> 00:11:24,820
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you were to stop resisting by default, the disagreements,

137
00:11:25,895 --> 00:11:35,603
[SPEAKER_00]: The admonishment, being told off, or being pointed as wrong, or maybe questioned even, the less frightening it will be to face that.

138
00:11:36,123 --> 00:11:45,030
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as I said, you can't just decide to start doing that rightly, despite of psychotherapy tries to teach you, and so on, and modern skillful communication management.

139
00:11:45,670 --> 00:11:51,215
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only start doing it rightly by removing the unsuitable qualities

140
00:11:52,438 --> 00:11:55,283
[SPEAKER_00]: Is your mind still gripped with desire, with lust, with ill-will, with...

141
00:12:05,692 --> 00:12:07,793
[SPEAKER_00]: Malice and and and so on.

142
00:12:07,813 --> 00:12:25,904
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, it's like, oh, by addressing those things, that's how you then get to face the criticism or face the admonishment and not automatically, you know, tighten up and need to need to retort, need to find the way out, need to question the accuser, rebuke him, retort or distract.

143
00:12:25,924 --> 00:12:27,725
[SPEAKER_00]: Just start throwing millions of other points

144
00:12:28,325 --> 00:12:36,435
[SPEAKER_00]: That you will not... you will not address the original point made against you for as long as all these other points that you now brought up on top of it are not addressed as well first.

145
00:12:36,675 --> 00:12:37,656
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's important.

146
00:12:37,696 --> 00:12:44,584
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just basically, you know, like a lawyer just throwing out technicalities of the law to mask the fact that he knows his client's wrong.

147
00:12:45,386 --> 00:12:51,709
[SPEAKER_00]: And he just tries to drag it on until everything gets bogged down in details, taken out of context, and that's how he sets him free.

148
00:12:52,630 --> 00:12:54,631
[SPEAKER_00]: So, people do that when, again...

149
00:12:54,791 --> 00:13:05,576
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they might not be attacking somebody personally, but they still refuse to acknowledge the admonishment, to acknowledge the disagreement, to see it for what it is, and then present their counter-argument.

150
00:13:06,557 --> 00:13:12,920
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you were truly... And the reason why people don't want to do that is because they're not used to clarifying their own thinking

151
00:13:13,989 --> 00:13:17,793
[SPEAKER_00]: With that, as I said, with that rational reflection of, okay, my view is such.

152
00:13:19,295 --> 00:13:20,897
[SPEAKER_00]: I hold that this is right and that's wrong.

153
00:13:21,177 --> 00:13:21,437
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

154
00:13:21,898 --> 00:13:23,840
[SPEAKER_00]: So, start questioning yourself already.

155
00:13:24,840 --> 00:13:29,904
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by the time you encounter disagreement in the world and other people questioning you, you would have already done that so many times.

156
00:13:30,324 --> 00:13:34,868
[SPEAKER_00]: And your views would have been fortified, not through, like, skilled avoidance and so on.

157
00:13:35,108 --> 00:13:49,380
[SPEAKER_00]: No, they would have been freed from contradictions, because if you start questioning yourself rightly, you will not have a problem in abandoning your views, if you discover discrepancies in it, if you find that it's contradictory, if you find that it's incomplete.

158
00:13:50,698 --> 00:13:57,026
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't be as shocked to find that out, if somebody else points it out, because you would have done that beforehand already.

159
00:13:57,106 --> 00:14:00,490
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's already the practice of reviewing your behavior.

160
00:14:01,883 --> 00:14:02,884
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you acting out of anger?

161
00:14:02,944 --> 00:14:03,885
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you acting out of your will?

162
00:14:04,245 --> 00:14:07,368
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just to kind of wallow in that, yes, I'm a bad person.

163
00:14:07,388 --> 00:14:26,125
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, just so that next time, when these things present, when these choices of acting out of evil wishes or desires or anger or arrogance, when those choices are presented, I can actually see them, as opposed to only in hindsight discover that, oh, yeah, I chose that and did that and said that.

164
00:14:27,348 --> 00:14:29,469
[SPEAKER_00]: And that takes, like, persistent reflection.

165
00:14:29,929 --> 00:14:34,970
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to reflect and invest effort into, like, dig this thing up, so to recognize them.

166
00:14:35,650 --> 00:14:39,911
[SPEAKER_00]: So to then be able to not choose them when the pressure is put on you.

167
00:14:40,732 --> 00:14:50,814
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if a person has already done that privately, when they encounter admonishment, disagreement, right or wrong, doesn't matter, they will have no problem in seeing it for what it is.

168
00:14:51,294 --> 00:14:54,015
[SPEAKER_00]: And if it's correct, it's like, oh, yeah, thank you for pointing that out.

169
00:14:55,192 --> 00:14:56,013
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't think about that.

170
00:14:56,653 --> 00:15:00,355
[SPEAKER_00]: If it's wrong, again, they won't gloat or feel superior or counter to it.

171
00:15:00,375 --> 00:15:02,976
[SPEAKER_00]: They just say, no, you're mistaken because of this, this and this.

172
00:15:03,556 --> 00:15:05,798
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your argument doesn't add up, not mine.

173
00:15:07,378 --> 00:15:09,760
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's that's exactly what he carries on.

174
00:15:10,380 --> 00:15:16,483
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he's attached to his own views.

175
00:15:16,503 --> 00:15:18,344
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, sorry, before that, yes.

176
00:15:18,584 --> 00:15:19,485
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he dodges the issue.

177
00:15:19,805 --> 00:15:21,726
[SPEAKER_00]: He's unable to account for the evidence.

178
00:15:23,801 --> 00:15:28,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, he denigrates, he's contentious, he's jealous, stingy, devious, deceitful, stubborn and arrogant.

179
00:15:29,022 --> 00:15:32,703
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, because he's attached to his own views, holding them tight and refusing to let go.

180
00:15:33,203 --> 00:15:36,204
[SPEAKER_00]: So, these are the qualities that make him hard to admonish.

181
00:15:36,244 --> 00:15:49,448
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, these are the qualities that it will make him unable to accept any form of... to accept the experience of disagreement without being affected by it.

182
00:15:49,868 --> 00:15:50,628
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's put it that way.

183
00:15:51,824 --> 00:15:55,685
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether disagreement or admonishment is right or wrong, doesn't matter.

184
00:15:56,025 --> 00:15:58,346
[SPEAKER_00]: He will first and foremost be affected by it.

185
00:15:59,006 --> 00:16:00,646
[SPEAKER_00]: Emotionally, mentally.

186
00:16:00,686 --> 00:16:08,788
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if he has a perfect management of coping, never says the wrong thing, internally, first and foremost, he will be affected by it.

187
00:16:08,908 --> 00:16:09,628
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a problem.

188
00:16:09,648 --> 00:16:20,091
[SPEAKER_00]: For somebody who wants to practice, somebody who wants to calm their mind and become accomplished in dhamma, any form of being affected by experience is work to do, is something that needs to be abandoned.

189
00:16:21,875 --> 00:16:26,836
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, the Buddha goes... the Mahamagrana goes and talks about the... what are the qualities that make him easy to admonish.

190
00:16:26,976 --> 00:16:27,816
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it's the opposite.

191
00:16:28,417 --> 00:16:34,118
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he doesn't... he doesn't have evil wishes, he's not attached to his own views and so on.

192
00:16:34,798 --> 00:16:38,539
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in such case, a bhikkhu should evaluate himself.

193
00:16:38,559 --> 00:16:40,199
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this person has evil wishes.

194
00:16:40,379 --> 00:16:42,420
[SPEAKER_00]: He's fallen under the sway of these wishes.

195
00:16:43,676 --> 00:16:45,117
[SPEAKER_00]: And I should not like...

196
00:16:45,257 --> 00:16:46,997
[SPEAKER_00]: I do not like or approve of that person.

197
00:16:47,017 --> 00:16:52,879
[SPEAKER_00]: And if I were to fall under the sway of evil wishes, others wouldn't like or approve of me.

198
00:16:53,479 --> 00:17:01,902
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, an impartial observer can always see in an argument, like, who is the rational one.

199
00:17:03,374 --> 00:17:14,363
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if not necessarily wrong, if somebody is truly impartial, they can see, oh, this person is affected by this, they're not making sense, they're going ad hominem attacking, or whatever.

200
00:17:15,003 --> 00:17:22,369
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, the sutta carries on that a bhikkhu should evaluate himself in this way, in regard to the evil wishes, anger, malice, arrogance,

201
00:17:23,390 --> 00:17:25,732
[SPEAKER_00]: Is he resorting to ad hominem attacks?

202
00:17:26,513 --> 00:17:28,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Is he dodging the issue, and so on?

203
00:17:28,895 --> 00:17:39,584
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, he says that... the sutta carries on, saying that, suppose, upon reflection, Bhikkhu recognizes these things in him, these unwholesome qualities that need to be abandoned, and he should make an effort to give them up.

204
00:17:41,205 --> 00:17:42,848
[SPEAKER_00]: So, see, that's another important point here.

205
00:17:43,348 --> 00:17:51,782
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like, you know, then he should practice this technique and do that or counter it with this experience and focus on that.

206
00:17:52,242 --> 00:17:52,383
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

207
00:17:52,964 --> 00:17:55,367
[SPEAKER_00]: He needs to evaluate

208
00:17:56,423 --> 00:18:13,326
[SPEAKER_00]: His mind, his experience, his past actions, past choices, recent or... doesn't matter how recent or not, he needs to detect, determine that there are still these unfavorable qualities in him, or that he's prone to them, or that he could engage with them, and then abandon that.

209
00:18:13,646 --> 00:18:16,107
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, abandon that through recognition.

210
00:18:16,687 --> 00:18:21,248
[SPEAKER_00]: Recognize if there is not enough negative value

211
00:18:22,521 --> 00:18:34,130
[SPEAKER_00]: Attached to these unwholesome qualities, through reflection, through repeated reflection, when those qualities present themselves, you still act out of them, you still choose them.

212
00:18:34,810 --> 00:18:43,957
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you, before they present themselves, you reflect frequently, and you are putting a negative value on those qualities,

213
00:18:45,900 --> 00:18:49,162
[SPEAKER_00]: When they present themselves, that value will be your mindfulness.

214
00:18:49,402 --> 00:18:57,606
[SPEAKER_00]: That negative value will be your container from preventing you to automatically just blurt things out, or act, or choose this or that, that's unwholesome.

215
00:18:58,386 --> 00:19:04,509
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, that's why the Buddha talked about, like, sense of shame and fear of wrongdoing are the guardians of the world.

216
00:19:05,259 --> 00:19:12,507
[SPEAKER_00]: So, these are like... sense of shame is basically a negative value attached to certain types of behavior that result in shame.

217
00:19:13,068 --> 00:19:17,272
[SPEAKER_00]: So, having sense of shame means that value protects you from going down the path of those behaviors.

218
00:19:17,733 --> 00:19:22,097
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignoring the sense of shame means going down that... the paths of those behavior.

219
00:19:22,698 --> 00:19:22,878
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

220
00:19:23,882 --> 00:19:30,507
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't actually need to, like, be constantly, sort of, dwelling in a sense of shame.

221
00:19:30,727 --> 00:19:33,489
[SPEAKER_00]: You just need to have that on the level of the value, that these things are bad.

222
00:19:34,329 --> 00:19:35,210
[SPEAKER_00]: I understood that.

223
00:19:35,510 --> 00:19:37,171
[SPEAKER_00]: I reflected frequently upon it.

224
00:19:37,231 --> 00:19:39,533
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now that negative value is attached to them.

225
00:19:40,298 --> 00:19:49,166
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when I'm mindless, distracted, and suddenly desires present themselves or real will, it will come with that negative value that I've been cultivating in regard to it.

226
00:19:49,646 --> 00:19:51,668
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how sense restraint starts bearing fruit.

227
00:19:51,989 --> 00:20:00,036
[SPEAKER_00]: You can be mindlessly restrained and keep all the precepts and some, and not give in to anything, but not allow that

228
00:20:01,865 --> 00:20:05,667
[SPEAKER_00]: The weight of those choices to come down to, like, on that level of intention.

229
00:20:06,287 --> 00:20:21,915
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to recognize that the precepts and saying no to certain things and so on, needs to be accompanied with a whole, like, a frequent reflection on the level of values, whereby I'm not just not doing this or that thing because some authority said so.

230
00:20:22,415 --> 00:20:25,936
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm doing it because it's wrong and it's for my harm, how to say.

231
00:20:27,197 --> 00:20:27,898
[SPEAKER_00]: But I don't see.

232
00:20:27,998 --> 00:20:28,838
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't see that harm.

233
00:20:28,898 --> 00:20:29,719
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't see why it's wrong.

234
00:20:29,779 --> 00:20:30,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

235
00:20:30,139 --> 00:20:31,660
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now you need to do the work.

236
00:20:32,200 --> 00:20:41,726
[SPEAKER_00]: And while still not doing it, you need to reflect, and dig deeper, and recollect, and bring back the harm, and discern it, layer by layer by layer.

237
00:20:41,866 --> 00:20:47,710
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you know, a year or so later, or longer, depending on how diligent you were, you wouldn't have to think about it.

238
00:20:48,170 --> 00:20:52,033
[SPEAKER_00]: When the desire to do such and such things presents itself, comes with the implication of harm.

239
00:20:53,647 --> 00:21:05,838
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it might feel like you need to fabricate the harm initially, and it's true for some people, but that's because it will work, because there is a factual harm in those things that's obscured.

240
00:21:06,498 --> 00:21:17,828
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if sensuality were truly not harmful, no amount of you fabricating harm and values of harm in regard to it would make it be... would make it imply harm, when it presents itself.

241
00:21:18,845 --> 00:21:20,006
[SPEAKER_00]: But it is actually harmful.

242
00:21:20,526 --> 00:21:21,847
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the part that's not seen.

243
00:21:22,507 --> 00:21:28,231
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, if you dwell and frequently reflect upon that, it will become part of sensuality.

244
00:21:28,291 --> 00:21:29,592
[SPEAKER_00]: That harm that's now seen.

245
00:21:29,612 --> 00:21:33,675
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how the suttas mean when they say the danger becomes apparent.

246
00:21:33,935 --> 00:21:36,757
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger that was always implicit wasn't apparent before.

247
00:21:37,337 --> 00:21:46,523
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, through withdrawal, frequent reflection upon it, trying to discern it, trying to see more of it, when it's there, oh, now it's apparent.

248
00:21:46,743 --> 00:21:47,944
[SPEAKER_00]: Now it's effortless to see it.

249
00:21:48,896 --> 00:21:54,641
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's how the monk says... because when the suttas say, oh, he just abandons these qualities, you think, so what?

250
00:21:55,161 --> 00:21:56,923
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, why didn't the Buddha say more?

251
00:21:57,283 --> 00:21:58,544
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you abandon those qualities?

252
00:21:59,545 --> 00:22:00,005
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you do this?

253
00:22:00,025 --> 00:22:00,626
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you do that?

254
00:22:00,666 --> 00:22:01,766
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you practice concentration?

255
00:22:01,786 --> 00:22:02,487
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you practice that?

256
00:22:02,547 --> 00:22:02,847
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

257
00:22:03,408 --> 00:22:12,075
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's just he recognizes them, he detects them for what they are, he sees harm in them, he attaches that negative value to them.

258
00:22:13,247 --> 00:22:17,591
[SPEAKER_00]: Through his own effort, through his own frequent recollection, reflection, and so on.

259
00:22:18,131 --> 00:22:21,755
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, when those things come up again, on their own, they come with that.

260
00:22:22,235 --> 00:22:25,358
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he becomes effortless to say no to them, eventually.

261
00:22:25,378 --> 00:22:27,620
[SPEAKER_00]: Not without a proper amount of work.

262
00:22:27,760 --> 00:22:29,982
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how you simply abandon them.

263
00:22:30,583 --> 00:22:38,510
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize the work is on the level of reshaping the value of... well, reshaping the value of acting out of sensuality, ill-will, distraction.

264
00:22:39,355 --> 00:22:48,000
[SPEAKER_00]: If you would have internally reshaped long enough, properly, thoroughly, through prior sense restraint, and guarding of the sense doors, and taming of the mind, it results in everything that follows.

265
00:22:48,100 --> 00:22:55,804
[SPEAKER_00]: It results in in taming of the senses, it results in calmness, it results in jhāna, it results in liberation.

266
00:22:55,844 --> 00:22:56,824
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's no other work.

267
00:22:57,725 --> 00:23:03,788
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why in the other... in the other Majjhima Nikaya Sutta, the similar charity we did, says purification of behavior

268
00:23:04,864 --> 00:23:06,664
[SPEAKER_00]: That leads to purification of the mind.

269
00:23:07,265 --> 00:23:08,765
[SPEAKER_00]: That leads to purification of the views.

270
00:23:09,285 --> 00:23:11,446
[SPEAKER_00]: That then leads to joy and liberation and so on.

271
00:23:12,426 --> 00:23:14,206
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's... there's nothing else there.

272
00:23:14,586 --> 00:23:27,429
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like that the Buddha omitted some crucial parts of the practice that only in contemporary Buddhism and commentaries we find all these techniques and so on and it's like it doesn't exist because there is no room for it.

273
00:23:28,570 --> 00:23:29,430
[SPEAKER_00]: It's unnecessary.

274
00:23:30,092 --> 00:23:43,286
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if you address the root problem, which is you willingly choosing these unskillful qualities that then make you irrational, make you resist any discomfort of disagreement, discrepancy and so on.

275
00:23:44,307 --> 00:23:45,849
[SPEAKER_00]: If you willingly stop choosing that,

276
00:23:47,829 --> 00:23:49,090
[SPEAKER_00]: Your views will be purified.

277
00:23:49,130 --> 00:23:57,876
[SPEAKER_00]: You will not be... you will not be obstinately acting out of those things and you will not continue to be affected by the disagreements or discrepancy.

278
00:23:57,916 --> 00:24:06,903
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, see, like, self-evaluating is already your own attitude of looking for disagreement with yourself.

279
00:24:08,684 --> 00:24:09,825
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's already the practice.

280
00:24:10,645 --> 00:24:14,168
[SPEAKER_00]: Reviewing your behavior, is there anything that shouldn't be there?

281
00:24:15,337 --> 00:24:18,618
[SPEAKER_00]: From, obviously, the value, the standpoint of somebody who wants to practice the dhamma.

282
00:24:20,118 --> 00:24:20,639
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh...

283
00:24:21,019 --> 00:24:22,679
[SPEAKER_00]: So, disagreement is good.

284
00:24:22,759 --> 00:24:23,660
[SPEAKER_00]: Discrimination is good.

285
00:24:25,260 --> 00:24:29,381
[SPEAKER_00]: Done on that right level, you discriminate against good behavior and bad behavior.

286
00:24:30,201 --> 00:24:32,462
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly how you bring yourself in line.

287
00:24:33,723 --> 00:24:34,383
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh...

288
00:24:35,698 --> 00:24:41,581
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not doing it on that personal level, that's why it's so uncomfortable when you encounter it coming at you externally.

289
00:24:42,161 --> 00:24:56,107
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, even if it's completely wrong, like, somebody can have some ridiculous argument, whatever, against your view, and you will still be affected by it, first and foremost, on that personal level, from a person who did that.

290
00:24:56,127 --> 00:24:56,808
[SPEAKER_00]: Although you know

291
00:24:57,815 --> 00:25:02,998
[SPEAKER_00]: You even have the correct, so to speak, argument against it.

292
00:25:03,098 --> 00:25:09,101
[SPEAKER_00]: They would show that, no, they're the irrational one, their argument is contradictory, yours isn't.

293
00:25:09,582 --> 00:25:13,164
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fact is that, on a personal level, you're affected by the experience of it.

294
00:25:13,624 --> 00:25:15,425
[SPEAKER_00]: To that extent, that is your wrongdoing.

295
00:25:15,465 --> 00:25:17,126
[SPEAKER_00]: That should not be happening either.

296
00:25:18,147 --> 00:25:20,128
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's... Of course, it's not going to be overnight.

297
00:25:20,832 --> 00:25:28,215
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, these these layers of irrationality and emotional response to the discomfort, it's not going to be solved just just in a magical finger snap.

298
00:25:29,015 --> 00:25:44,922
[SPEAKER_00]: But you need to recognize that for as long as there is a degree of that mental resistance towards such experience of admonishment, being rebuked, being disagreed with, being pointed out, things that that you don't necessarily want to hear and so on, it's like there is absolutely no reason to be any any resistance towards it.

299
00:25:45,967 --> 00:25:52,812
[SPEAKER_00]: And if there is no resistance towards it, then, as I said, you you will have no problem in... because you would... you will not lose perspective.

300
00:25:53,112 --> 00:25:55,473
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you would immediately see through whatever's presented at you.

301
00:25:56,614 --> 00:25:58,075
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, oh, yeah, I did not consider that.

302
00:25:59,236 --> 00:25:59,576
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you.

303
00:25:59,736 --> 00:26:03,479
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're easy to admonish and then other people would be willing.

304
00:26:03,519 --> 00:26:13,546
[SPEAKER_00]: And see, like, say, if somebody who also practices dhamma, who is actually more... maybe developed even more than you are, in taming the mind and so on, points things at you,

305
00:26:14,532 --> 00:26:22,518
[SPEAKER_00]: From their better standpoint, from their greater development of their mind, and you are not irrationally resisting it, they'll do it again.

306
00:26:22,758 --> 00:26:24,859
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's for your benefit, factually.

307
00:26:24,919 --> 00:26:26,741
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what this sutta specifically talks about.

308
00:26:27,181 --> 00:26:29,923
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as I said, you can apply that in much broader sense.

309
00:26:30,063 --> 00:26:39,290
[SPEAKER_00]: And even if they're not speaking for your welfare, they're just criticizing you mindlessly, you can use that to see, oh, but am I affected by that, first and foremost?

310
00:26:39,790 --> 00:26:47,797
[SPEAKER_00]: Regardless of, like, how ridiculous the argument might be, and so on, or whether you are right or wrong, it's just, is this experience, in and of itself, affecting me?

311
00:26:48,057 --> 00:26:54,783
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, do I feel, like, a little, at least, twitch towards, hmm, I want to put them down, or I want this, or I want that.

312
00:26:54,803 --> 00:26:56,724
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, do I remain unmoved?

313
00:26:57,965 --> 00:27:00,528
[SPEAKER_00]: And, and that's, that's again, oh, I'm moved, there you go.

314
00:27:00,788 --> 00:27:06,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Re-evaluate that, and then see, because there won't be any other qualities because of which you are moved.

315
00:27:06,473 --> 00:27:07,493
[SPEAKER_00]: It's what the sutta lists.

316
00:27:08,303 --> 00:27:20,448
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, if I'm moved, it has to be only because I still choose a degree of sensuality, I still choose ill-will, annoyance, blurting out things, I still don't restrain my speech well enough.

317
00:27:20,628 --> 00:27:21,088
[SPEAKER_00]: There you go.

318
00:27:21,488 --> 00:27:22,989
[SPEAKER_00]: Develop one, develop two.

319
00:27:24,590 --> 00:27:29,393
[SPEAKER_00]: And, there will be no more movement, even when such experience comes your way.

320
00:27:30,453 --> 00:27:36,777
[SPEAKER_00]: And, again, if there is no more movement, there is no... there is no shift, there is no obscuring of the perspective that you have.

321
00:27:37,397 --> 00:27:43,060
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, you would immediately see whether that person... Because, sometimes, somebody might even criticize you while they actually agree with you.

322
00:27:43,943 --> 00:27:48,565
[SPEAKER_00]: But they didn't quite understand what you were saying, so they're kind of saying the same, because they misunderstood you.

323
00:27:49,105 --> 00:27:53,827
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, if you were affected by it, you're gonna start arguing back, without even actually...

324
00:27:53,847 --> 00:27:54,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Hold on.

325
00:27:54,468 --> 00:27:56,308
[SPEAKER_00]: But they are saying the same, just in different words.

326
00:27:57,189 --> 00:28:01,531
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you would get to recognize that, and clarify the thing, and it would be for the benefit of them.

327
00:28:03,247 --> 00:28:06,649
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all of that, it's like, see, sometimes a person might want to try.

328
00:28:06,669 --> 00:28:08,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so I'll start practicing all these things.

329
00:28:08,769 --> 00:28:19,133
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll start practicing not disagreeing, and not doing this, and not being affected by it, and not going at homonym attacks, and I will only listen to the reason, and I'll present this.

330
00:28:19,933 --> 00:28:24,035
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can be partially successful, but all of that is the symptom of the issue.

331
00:28:24,679 --> 00:28:31,324
[SPEAKER_00]: The issue being, you still act out of desire, you're still acting out of ill-will, you're still acting out of distraction, and everything else that comes out of it.

332
00:28:32,204 --> 00:28:33,886
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to address it, really?

333
00:28:34,626 --> 00:28:39,009
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Sutta says, you evaluate mind for those unskillful qualities, are they still there?

334
00:28:39,750 --> 00:28:53,500
[SPEAKER_00]: If you recognize them, dwell day and night on attaching unwholesome, bad value to them, so when they come up, you immediately see them, and you will not be perpetually choosing the course of action that's rooted in them.

